    "narrative": "The LawDog lineup is anchored today by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (79.96) and **Lawrence Butler** (74.61), our clear-cut elite plays. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a highly favorable matchup against George Klassen (5.56 xERA), providing the highest ceiling despite the marginal tax applied for the opposing catcher\u2019s pop time. **Lawrence Butler** is our most efficient start, leveraging a +10% park factor and a +13% platoon edge against Zebby Matthews, making him a prime candidate to anchor our daily production. Meanwhile, **JJ Bleday** (65.58) provides surprising value against the shaky matchup of Carson Whisenhunt; his massive +14% platoon edge effectively neutralizes his lower batting position, keeping him safely in the strong-start tier.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Gavin Sheets** (40.24), who hovered near the threshold but ultimately failed to crack the starting nine. While his +7% platoon edge against Braxton Ashcraft provided some upside, the combination of a -18% SP skill penalty and a lack of elite speed\u2014a critical metric for our roster construction\u2014pushed him below our floor requirements. We are opting to lean into the speed and situational utility of **Justin Crawford** and **Jung Hoo Lee** instead. \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding our heavy exposure to Logan Gilbert (3.46 xERA), who opposes **Luis Arraez**, **Bryce Harper**, and **Justin Crawford** today. While **Bryce Harper** maintains a solid 53.30 score due to his high xwOBA, the elite arm of Gilbert is a significant drag on our efficiency. Furthermore, the lineup status for **Cal Raleigh** remains \"Pending\"; should he shift lower than the assumed #4 spot, his already precarious 42.78 score could dip toward the benching zone to preserve our daily game caps.",
